WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.426
Welcome to this Java tutorial, where you'll

00:00:03.426 --> 00:00:06.227
learn to read keyboard input with Scanner and

00:00:06.227 --> 00:00:08.903
choose methods for text, whole numbers, and

00:00:08.903 --> 00:00:09.837
decimal values.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:12.991
Interactive programs can respond to new

00:00:12.991 --> 00:00:14.729
information while they run.

00:00:14.729 --> 00:00:17.625
Reading text and parsing numbers, which means

00:00:17.625 --> 00:00:20.522
converting numeric text into a numeric value,

00:00:20.522 --> 00:00:23.354
lets software collect requests and calculate

00:00:23.354 --> 00:00:26.380
useful results without editing its source code.

00:00:26.919 --> 00:00:29.449
A campus workshop assistant needs a small

00:00:29.449 --> 00:00:30.436
receipt program.

00:00:30.436 --> 00:00:33.151
The person at the keyboard will enter a full

00:00:33.151 --> 00:00:35.434
attendee name, a one-word kit code, a

00:00:35.434 --> 00:00:38.272
whole-number quantity, a dollar price per kit,

00:00:38.272 --> 00:00:39.630
and a pickup location.

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:42.530
We'll ask for one response on each line and use

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:44.319
a decimal point in the price.

00:00:44.864 --> 00:00:47.632
The program should display the attendee, kit

00:00:47.632 --> 00:00:50.527
quantity and code, calculated cost, and pickup

00:00:50.527 --> 00:00:51.093
location.

00:00:51.093 --> 00:00:53.798
Names and locations must keep their spaces.

00:00:53.798 --> 00:00:56.441
We'll run the completed program twice with

00:00:56.441 --> 00:00:59.335
different keyboard responses to check that the

00:00:59.335 --> 00:01:01.789
same instructions handle both requests.

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:05.376
Let's open Main.java in the Workspace and build

00:01:05.376 --> 00:01:06.663
the receipt program.

00:01:06.663 --> 00:01:09.303
Main is the class that contains our code.

00:01:09.303 --> 00:01:12.135
Its main method is the named operation where

00:01:12.135 --> 00:01:13.423
this program starts.

00:01:13.423 --> 00:01:16.127
We'll put our input and receipt statements

00:01:16.127 --> 00:01:17.350
inside that method.

00:01:28.994 --> 00:01:31.501
Before we can ask for responses, we need a

00:01:31.501 --> 00:01:33.470
reader connected to the keyboard.

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:36.096
The import makes the Scanner type available.

00:01:36.096 --> 00:01:38.603
The word new creates a Scanner object, and

00:01:38.603 --> 00:01:41.229
System.in is standard input, the stream that

00:01:41.229 --> 00:01:43.557
receives what we type into this running

00:01:43.557 --> 00:01:44.094
terminal.

00:01:49.291 --> 00:01:52.018
Our keyboard variable now refers to that reader.

00:01:52.018 --> 00:01:54.744
A prompt is a message that tells the person what

00:01:54.744 --> 00:01:55.255
to enter.

00:01:55.255 --> 00:01:57.698
We'll print the name prompt before reading.

00:01:57.698 --> 00:02:00.368
The nextLine method returns a String containing

00:02:00.368 --> 00:02:02.697
the rest of a line, including spaces, and

00:02:02.697 --> 00:02:04.458
consumes its ending line break.

00:02:04.458 --> 00:02:06.957
The trim method removes spaces only from the

00:02:06.957 --> 00:02:08.491
ends of that returned name.

00:02:16.382 --> 00:02:18.472
This read waits when no complete line is

00:02:18.472 --> 00:02:18.994
available.

00:02:18.994 --> 00:02:21.241
Pressing Enter completes our name response.

00:02:21.241 --> 00:02:23.748
A kit code has a different shape: it must be one

00:02:23.748 --> 00:02:24.010
word.

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:26.204
A token is one piece of input separated by

00:02:26.204 --> 00:02:28.398
whitespace, such as spaces or line breaks.

00:02:28.398 --> 00:02:30.436
The next method returns a String token.

00:02:30.436 --> 00:02:32.943
It skips leading whitespace and stops before the

00:02:32.943 --> 00:02:35.190
whitespace after that token, so it does not

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:36.757
return a whole multiword line.

00:02:44.038 --> 00:02:46.746
Quantity must support arithmetic, so a String

00:02:46.746 --> 00:02:47.949
token is not enough.

00:02:47.949 --> 00:02:50.778
The nextInt method skips leading whitespace and

00:02:50.778 --> 00:02:52.944
parses the next token into an int, a

00:02:52.944 --> 00:02:54.087
whole-number value.

00:02:54.087 --> 00:02:56.976
The parsing happens before the value is assigned

00:02:56.976 --> 00:02:57.698
to quantity.

00:02:57.698 --> 00:03:00.346
A word such as three would fail this numeric

00:03:00.346 --> 00:03:02.813
read; we'll study input validation later.

00:03:09.982 --> 00:03:12.699
The price can include a fraction of a dollar.

00:03:12.699 --> 00:03:15.596
The nextDouble method parses the next token into

00:03:15.596 --> 00:03:16.502
a double value.

00:03:16.502 --> 00:03:19.399
For this Workspace, enter a decimal point, as in

00:03:19.399 --> 00:03:20.365
four point five.

00:03:20.365 --> 00:03:23.142
Both numeric methods leave the delimiter after

00:03:23.142 --> 00:03:24.289
their token unread.

00:03:24.289 --> 00:03:26.704
A delimiter is a separator; Scanner uses

00:03:26.704 --> 00:03:28.032
whitespace by default.

00:03:40.584 --> 00:03:42.751
Here is the boundary we must handle.

00:03:42.751 --> 00:03:45.519
After the price token, pressing Enter has also

00:03:45.519 --> 00:03:46.844
supplied a line break.

00:03:46.844 --> 00:03:49.733
The next line read would return the rest of that

00:03:49.733 --> 00:03:52.441
price line, which is empty when we typed only

00:03:52.441 --> 00:03:53.043
the price.

00:03:53.043 --> 00:03:55.751
We consume that remainder first, then ask for

00:03:55.751 --> 00:03:58.159
the pickup location on its own new line.

00:04:07.748 --> 00:04:10.440
The extra nextLine call does not read the future

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:11.338
pickup response.

00:04:11.338 --> 00:04:13.301
It finishes the current price line.

00:04:13.301 --> 00:04:15.993
If meaningful text followed a number on the same

00:04:15.993 --> 00:04:18.629
line, this call would consume that text too, so

00:04:18.629 --> 00:04:20.816
don't discard a line remainder blindly.

00:04:20.816 --> 00:04:23.340
Our prompts deliberately request one response

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:23.845
per line.

00:04:23.845 --> 00:04:26.201
The following nextLine keeps spaces in the

00:04:26.201 --> 00:04:27.098
pickup location.

00:04:27.651 --> 00:04:30.308
With both numeric values available, we can

00:04:30.308 --> 00:04:32.966
multiply quantity by price and display the

00:04:32.966 --> 00:04:33.472
receipt.

00:04:33.472 --> 00:04:36.446
We'll combine the stored text and numbers using

00:04:36.446 --> 00:04:39.420
the printing and String skills from the earlier

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:39.926
lessons.

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:04.432
The calculation uses numeric values; the print

00:05:04.432 --> 00:05:07.223
statements then turn the results into readable

00:05:07.223 --> 00:05:07.770
messages.

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:10.683
Let's run the completed program with the toolbar

00:05:10.683 --> 00:05:11.411
play button.

00:05:11.411 --> 00:05:13.778
For each visible prompt, I'll focus the

00:05:13.778 --> 00:05:16.388
terminal, type a response, and press Enter.

00:05:16.388 --> 00:05:19.240
Watch how each read waits for its own response.

00:05:44.939 --> 00:05:47.556
Maya's full name and Student Center both kept

00:05:47.556 --> 00:05:48.312
their spaces.

00:05:48.312 --> 00:05:50.987
Three art kits at four dollars and fifty cents

00:05:50.987 --> 00:05:53.721
each produced thirteen dollars and fifty cents.

00:05:53.721 --> 00:05:56.512
Java displays that double as thirteen point five

00:05:56.512 --> 00:05:58.722
here; currency formatting comes later.

00:05:58.722 --> 00:06:01.339
The pickup prompt waited for our new line, so

00:06:01.339 --> 00:06:03.666
the leftover price line did not skip the

00:06:03.666 --> 00:06:04.189
location.

00:06:04.789 --> 00:06:07.679
Now apply the same reasoning to another request.

00:06:07.679 --> 00:06:10.388
Without changing the source, we'll enter Nora

00:06:10.388 --> 00:06:13.278
Patel, art, four kits, a price of one point five

00:06:13.278 --> 00:06:14.843
dollars, and Library Desk.

00:06:14.843 --> 00:06:17.552
Predict the total and whether the full pickup

00:06:17.552 --> 00:06:18.817
location will appear.

00:06:18.817 --> 00:06:21.586
Pause the video to make your prediction before

00:06:21.586 --> 00:06:22.489
the second run.

00:06:52.847 --> 00:06:55.851
The second receipt shows Nora Patel, four art

00:06:55.851 --> 00:06:58.989
kits, a total of six dollars, and Library Desk.

00:06:58.989 --> 00:07:01.192
Four times one point five is six.

00:07:01.192 --> 00:07:04.263
The source stayed unchanged; only the keyboard

00:07:04.263 --> 00:07:05.465
responses changed.

00:07:05.465 --> 00:07:08.335
We have tested two real requests, including

00:07:08.335 --> 00:07:10.672
spaces in both names and locations.

00:07:11.274 --> 00:07:13.737
Scanner connected our program to standard input.

00:07:13.737 --> 00:07:16.148
We chose nextLine for whole-line text, next for

00:07:16.148 --> 00:07:18.251
a single token, nextInt for quantity, and

00:07:18.251 --> 00:07:19.329
nextDouble for price.

00:07:19.329 --> 00:07:21.792
Handling the price line remainder let the pickup

00:07:21.792 --> 00:07:22.818
read wait correctly.

00:07:22.818 --> 00:07:25.229
For another workshop, which fields would need a

00:07:25.229 --> 00:07:27.486
whole-line read, and where would you need to

00:07:27.486 --> 00:07:29.949
account for a token read's remaining line break?
